-- `IA.mth : a -> Int -> Int` (dispatch at argument 0) and `IZ.mth : Int -> b -> Int`
-- (dispatch at argument 1) are both admitted here, each through its own interface
-- import, and `fmodI.mth` is the imported standalone they shadow.  This is the graph of
-- `test/shadow_fixtures/i28_importer_two_admitted_agree`, with the occurrence
-- under-applied: `mth "abc"` supplies IA's receiver but not IZ's.  IZ's own impl
-- query cannot be asked at this occurrence, so the two declarations are not shown to
-- agree, and nothing else decides between them.  Spec answer (SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md
-- S2-DECL (d) bullet 2, ruled 2026-10-01): the located reject
-- `T-AMBIGUOUS-SHADOW-DECL` at `mth`, exit 1, in every import order.  Not 99 (the
-- fully applied i28 value), not 7 (`IZ String`'s body, which neither declaration's
-- own receiver reaches), and not `Type mismatch` (one declaration's scheme typing the
-- occurrence).
import fmodI.{mth}
import amodI.{IA, af}
import zmodI.{IZ, zf}

g : Int -> Int
g = mth "abc"

main = println (g 5 + af 0 + zf 0)
